INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '08b8b7ff-a37f-5dd7-b22e-ac903e29b901', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "Server optimization: find the bottleneck before upgrading", "keywords": "server optimization, web performance, TTFB, compression, images, VPS", "description": "A practical measurement sequence covering loopback requests, TTFB, compression, image weight and fair CDN comparisons."}, "slug": "server-optimization-measure-first", "title": "Server optimization: measure first, change second", "excerpt": "An internal response around 3 ms does not explain a user''s full wait. Separate processing, network time and asset loading before upgrading a server.", "body_html": "<p>“How do we make the website faster?” is often answered before anyone measures it. Teams add CPU or RAM, then discover that the page still feels slow.</p><p>This article follows the measurements recorded in our project draft, explains what they suggest, and identifies what they cannot establish.</p><h2>The system described</h2><p><em>The original draft dates these observations to 18 October 2026. The underlying logs are not available in this folder for independent verification. These are observations from one configuration, not a representative benchmark; repeat them under realistic load.</em></p><ul><li><p>A VPS with four CPU cores and 2.9 GB of RAM in Vietnam.</p></li><li><p>A Node server-rendered public site, static admin interface, Go API and PostgreSQL 16.</p></li><li><p>Another company&#39;s application sharing the same server.</p></li></ul><h2>Step 1: measure internally</h2><p>A slow page can combine application work, network time, CDN behavior and large assets. Start by separating them.</p><p>A loopback request removes the external network from the measurement. The draft records:</p><pre><code>Internal SSR:          3.1 ms   3.3 ms   2.9 ms\\nInternal /members API: 5.1 ms\\nInternal /menus API:   4.3 ms\\nInternal /settings:    3.7 ms\\nLoad average:         0.06\\nFree RAM:             1.9 GB</code></pre><p>These samples show <strong>no obvious server bottleneck at that moment</strong>. A load average of 0.06 does not mean the CPU is idle 99% of the time. Check CPU, I/O, memory, concurrency and p95/p99 latency under load before drawing a capacity conclusion.</p><p>Even a short internal measurement is valuable because it tells you where to investigate next.</p><h2>Step 2: measure from the user&#39;s location</h2><pre><code>From a user machine in Vietnam, direct connection:\\n  TLS handshake: 0.06–0.08 s\\n  TTFB:          0.08–0.10 s\\n  Total:         0.10–0.13 s</code></pre><p>Do not subtract an internal response time from an external total and call the remainder “network delay.” Use the same URL and content. Separate DNS, TCP, TLS, TTFB and body download; repeat measurements while controlling cache state and connection reuse. Also record whether a timing is an individual phase or a cumulative timestamp.</p><h2>Step 3: weigh the page&#39;s assets</h2><p>The draft&#39;s largest opportunities were outside application processing:</p><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Asset</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Original</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Compressed or converted</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p><code>entry-client.js</code></p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>482 KB</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>152 KB</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p><code>esm.js</code></p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>318 KB</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Not recorded</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>CSS</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>135 KB</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>30 KB</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>One profile image</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>697 KB</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>516 KB, WebP</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p><code>logo.png</code></p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>140 KB</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Not recorded</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p><code>favicon.png</code></p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>49 KB</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Not recorded</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>18 font files</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>290 KB</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Not recorded</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>That profile image is larger than the compressed <code>entry-client.js</code> file. The table does not establish the total size of all compressed JavaScript. Even so, 516 KB deserves attention for an image displayed about 320 pixels wide.</p><p>The draft also reports that adding <code>?w=320</code> still returned the original image. A resizing parameter is useful only if the server actually implements it.</p><p>A reasonable investigation order is images, compression, static asset caching, JavaScript, then hardware when CPU, memory or I/O measurements justify it. Let your actual page data change that order when necessary.</p><h2>Step 4: verify compression on the response</h2><p>An nginx configuration containing <code>gzip on;</code> is not proof that every intended asset is compressed. The gzip module supports <code>text/html</code>; CSS and JavaScript need appropriate MIME types in <code>gzip_types</code>.</p><p>Ask for a compressed response and inspect the transferred size:</p><pre><code>curl -s -o /dev/null --compressed -w &#39;%{size_download}&#39; https://your-domain/</code></pre><p>Check <code>Content-Encoding</code> too, and compare the same response without compression. A smaller body alone does not identify the algorithm. The draft records roughly 91 KB of HTML and about 21 KB transferred, but no original header is available here to verify its earlier attribution to zstd.</p><h2>Step 5: avoid a convenient but misleading test</h2><p>The first CDN test in the draft ran from the origin server itself. It recorded 348–838 ms, but followed origin → internet → CDN → origin: a route different from a real user&#39;s request.</p><p>A later client-side test recorded 0.31–0.49 seconds. However, the direct and CDN routes then tested different applications. Their difference cannot quantify the CDN&#39;s overhead.</p><p>Measure where users are, and compare like with like.</p><figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 480\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"480\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Legs of one request and their measurements\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"480\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Where a request goes — and what each leg measures</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"56\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"91\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Browser → network</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"111\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Measured from a real user&#39;s machine</text><text x=\\"748\\" y=\\"102\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">100–130 ms</text><path d=\\"M394.0 125.0 L400.0 131.0 L406.0 125.0\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"#9fb0c6\\" stroke-width=\\"1.5\\"></path><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"132\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"56\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"155\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">nginx</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"175\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Forwarding</text><text x=\\"748\\" y=\\"166\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">under 1 ms</text><path d=\\"M394.0 189.0 L400.0 195.0 L406.0 189.0\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"#9fb0c6\\" stroke-width=\\"1.5\\"></path><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"196\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"56\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"219\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Application (SSR / API)</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"239\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Measured on loopback</text><text x=\\"748\\" y=\\"230\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">3 ms</text><path d=\\"M394.0 253.0 L400.0 259.0 L406.0 253.0\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"#9fb0c6\\" stroke-width=\\"1.5\\"></path><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"260\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"56\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"283\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">PostgreSQL</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"303\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Already inside the number above</text><text x=\\"748\\" y=\\"294\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">under 2 ms</text><path d=\\"M394.0 317.0 L400.0 323.0 L406.0 317.0\\" fill=\\"none\\" stroke=\\"#9fb0c6\\" stroke-width=\\"1.5\\"></path><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"324\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"56\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"347\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Static assets</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"367\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Images, JavaScript, fonts</text><text x=\\"748\\" y=\\"358\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">not measured yet</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"412\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">The server answers in 3 ms on loopback; the user waits 100–130 ms. Those are</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"432\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">two measurements taken under different conditions — measure the network and</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"452\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">the assets separately before attributing the wait to one cause.</text></svg></figure><h2>Step 6: use a control measurement</h2><p>The project account describes certificate issuance failing with a connection reset. Packet capture reportedly showed no incoming SYN on port 80 from an overseas network, while a domestic connection reached the server normally.</p><p>That comparison helps locate a possible routing or filtering problem before the server. It does not by itself prove the hosting provider intentionally blocked the port.</p><p>The useful lesson is broader: <strong>a measurement that finds nothing needs a control that finds something</strong>. Otherwise you cannot distinguish absence of traffic from a faulty capture or test setup.</p><h2>A practical sequence</h2><ol><li><p>Measure loopback responses.</p></li><li><p>Measure from real user locations.</p></li><li><p>Sort assets by transferred size.</p></li><li><p>Check compression headers and bytes.</p></li><li><p>Inspect edge cache status.</p></li><li><p>Evaluate hardware when the evidence points there.</p></li></ol><hr><p><em>Figures are retained from the Zukatek draft for October 2026 and have not been independently reproduced in this review. Keep the method, and save the logs of your own measurements.</em></p><p>Reference: <a href=\\"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_gzip_module.html\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">nginx gzip module</a>.</p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '1d064338-6db9-51ba-9517-f591436107b2', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "Three small-team problems to solve before buying software", "keywords": "small team management, productivity, automation, customer management", "description": "Practical steps for shared knowledge, repetitive tasks and customer ownership in teams of 3–20 people."}, "slug": "three-small-team-problems", "title": "Three problems small teams face—and how to tackle them before buying software", "excerpt": "Scattered knowledge, repetitive work and missed customer inquiries often need a clearer process before they need another subscription.", "body_html": "<p>Many small teams I have worked with encounter these three problems. Their symptoms differ, but buying software before fixing the process can simply create another disorganized place with a monthly fee.</p><h2>Problem 1: knowledge lives in people&#39;s heads</h2><h3>Symptoms</h3><ul><li><p>“Ask Nam; he knows about that.”</p></li><li><p>One person&#39;s leave brings a whole area of work to a halt.</p></li><li><p>Three people give three different answers to the same customer question.</p></li></ul><h3>Why it happens</h3><p>People are not necessarily hiding information. At the moment of capture, writing something down costs more effort than remembering it. The person who knows the answer does not need documentation, so the cost falls on someone who joins later.</p><h3>What to do</h3><p>Start with a smaller rule than “write documentation”: <strong>when answering a question for the second time, write the answer down before sending it</strong>.</p><p>The first question is ordinary conversation. The second is evidence that the answer will be useful again. This avoids asking everyone to predict in advance what deserves documentation.</p><h3>When a tool becomes useful</h3><p>When people can no longer find the answers already recorded. A clear symptom is someone asking a documented question because search failed them.</p><p>Choose for search quality, shareable links, easy editing, then appearance. Attractive pages do little good if nobody can retrieve the answer.</p><h2>Problem 2: small repetitive tasks quietly consume time</h2><h3>Symptoms</h3><ul><li><p>Copying data between places.</p></li><li><p>Opening five tabs each morning to check what happened overnight.</p></li><li><p>“It only takes two minutes.”</p></li></ul><p>Two minutes, five times a day, over 200 working days adds up to 2,000 minutes: <strong>more than 33 hours a year</strong>, for one person doing one task.</p><h3>Why it stays unsolved</h3><p>Each repetition feels cheap. The cost becomes visible only when someone adds it up.</p><h3>What to do</h3><p>For one week, record the tasks you do more than once. A task name and a count are enough to start. At the end of the week, multiply by the time per repetition.</p><p>Use the totals to identify the first candidate for improvement rather than trying to automate everything at once.</p><h3>Before automating</h3><p>The order is <strong>remove, simplify, then automate</strong>.</p><p>Before scripting a weekly report, ask who reads it. If nobody does, the better solution may be to stop producing it.</p><h2>Problem 3: customer inquiries fall through the gaps</h2><h3>Symptoms</h3><ul><li><p>A weekend inquiry is not seen until Monday.</p></li><li><p>A potential customer asks a question, but nobody owns the reply.</p></li><li><p>Nobody knows how many inquiries arrived last month.</p></li></ul><h3>Why it happens</h3><p>Messages arrive somewhere without a clear person responsible for checking it. A shared inbox is a familiar example: everyone can see it, so everyone assumes someone else is dealing with it.</p><p>The project draft describes contact and newsletter forms that saved records correctly but notified nobody. The information waited until someone remembered to open the admin screen.</p><h3>What to do, in order</h3><p><strong>Assign one owner per channel.</strong> Use a person&#39;s name, not “the team.” Ownership can rotate weekly, but it should be clear at any given time.</p><p><strong>Notify proactively.</strong> Do not rely on someone remembering to check a screen. Define coverage hours and a fallback if the alert is missed.</p><p><strong>Count.</strong> Track inquiries received, replies made and response time. Without measurements it is hard to know whether the process is working.</p><h3>Keep alerts useful</h3><p>Group related messages. Five lines from a customer may be five machine events but one conversation to a person. Too many alerts teach people to turn them off.</p><p>Send alerts to people who can act on them and have permission to open the record. An alert leading to a locked screen is rarely useful.</p><figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 358\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"358\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Three small-team problems and the order to solve them\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"358\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Three problems — solve them in this order</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"106.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">3</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"94\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Customers fall through the gaps</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"116\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">The only damage measured in money</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"108\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">cheapest to fix → do it first</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"180.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">2</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"168\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Repeated work eats the hours</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"190\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">2 min × 5 times × 200 days = 33 hours</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"182\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">frees the time the next step needs</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"254.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">1</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"242\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">The knowledge is in people&#39;s heads</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"264\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">The most important one long term</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"256\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">slowest to show results → do it last</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"314\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">None of the three is a tooling problem. Buying software before fixing the way</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"334\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">you work just adds another mess, with a monthly fee.</text></svg></figure><h2>A suggested order</h2><p>If all three apply:</p><ol><li><p>Start with missed inquiries: they can directly affect revenue, and assigning an owner can be inexpensive.</p></li><li><p>Address repetitive work next to release time for other improvements.</p></li><li><p>Build shared knowledge steadily; its value often appears over a longer period.</p></li></ol><p>Change this order when your own costs or risks point elsewhere.</p><h2>When to buy software</h2><p>Answer three questions first:</p><ol><li><p>What is the current process? Can you describe it on one page?</p></li><li><p>Which steps should change or disappear before digitization?</p></li><li><p>What is the specific problem, and how will you measure improvement?</p></li></ol><p>“We should look more professional” is not yet a defined business problem. “Three inquiries a week go unanswered, each with an estimated value” gives you a problem to solve and a way to evaluate the result.</p><hr><p><em>Project situations are retained from the supplied draft and were not independently verified from logs. The proposed priorities are suggestions; adapt them to your team&#39;s costs and risks.</em></p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '32e26f10-59c0-561b-afb8-fb840106e5d8', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "Reduce fabricated business chatbot answers", "keywords": "AI chatbot, hallucination, business assistant, RAG, output validation", "description": "How prompts, data minimization, controlled retrieval, output validation and safe rendering address different chatbot risks."}, "slug": "reducing-chatbot-fabrications", "title": "Reducing fabricated chatbot answers through application design", "excerpt": "An assistant listed six convincing employees who did not exist. Stronger instructions alone were not enough; retrieval and application checks matter too.", "body_html": "<p>When we first enabled the website assistant, we asked a straightforward question: “Who is on the team?”</p><p>It listed six people, complete with names, titles and convincing descriptions. The actual team had four people. None of the six names was real.</p><h2>Why another instruction is not enough</h2><p>The instinctive response is to add: “Never invent people&#39;s names.” That can help, but it is not an application-enforced guarantee. A differently worded question can still produce an unsupported answer.</p><p>Keep this distinction clear: <strong>a model cannot accurately quote data it has not received, but it can still invent data it has not received</strong>.</p><p>The design question is therefore how the application can prevent unsupported claims from reaching the user, rather than how emphatically it can ask the model to behave.</p><h2>Three complementary controls</h2><h3>1. Instructions</h3><pre><code>Do not invent people&#39;s names, team sizes or roles.</code></pre><p>We retain instructions like this, while documenting their limitations. A guardrail people wrongly believe to be certain is especially dangerous.</p><h3>2. Minimize sensitive context</h3><p>Suppose the assistant is not allowed to quote prices. Keeping the internal price list out of its context reduces the risk of exposing that particular list. It does not stop the model inventing a plausible price.</p><p>If the product must not give quotations, the application needs to intercept that intent or return an approved fixed response. Output checks, tool access controls and retrieved-data permissions still matter.</p><p><strong>Data minimization and fabrication prevention solve different problems.</strong></p><h3>3. Retrieve facts and validate their use</h3><p>For information the assistant needs, such as approved products or staff, provide a controlled lookup rather than relying on a static prompt:</p><pre><code>Use tool results for product and team information.\\nIf the tool does not provide it, say that the information is unavailable.\\nUse list_members for team questions.</code></pre><p>A tool declaration alone does not ensure the model will call it. It may still answer from its own generated assumptions, as happened with the six names.</p><p>A stronger design makes the lookup mandatory for that question type, validates IDs and counts, and refuses or hands off when evidence is missing. For a staff list, the application can render approved records directly instead of asking the model to rewrite names.</p><p>These controls are not a universal ladder of certainty. They address different risks and work best together.</p><figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 376\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"376\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Three layers of control over an AI assistant&#39;s answers\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"376\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Three layers of control — each stops a different risk</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"78\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"6\\" height=\\"78\\" rx=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"94\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Layer 1 · An instruction in the prompt</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"115\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">“Never invent a person&#39;s name.”</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"135\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">WEAK — a request made to a probabilistic system</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"156\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"78\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"156\\" width=\\"6\\" height=\\"78\\" rx=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"182\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Layer 2 · Keep sensitive data out of the context</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"203\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">It cannot reveal what it was never given.</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"223\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">LESS EXPOSURE — but it does not stop invention</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"244\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"78\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"244\\" width=\\"6\\" height=\\"78\\" rx=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></rect><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"270\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Layer 3 · Require a lookup, then check it in the app</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"291\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Verify ids and counts against the tool&#39;s result.</text><text x=\\"52\\" y=\\"311\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">STRONGEST — declaring a tool is not enough on its own</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"356\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">The three layers address three different risks; they are not one scale of safety.</text></svg></figure><h2>Smaller decisions that still matter</h2><h3>Avoid wide tables in a narrow chat</h3><p>Our chat panel is about 360 pixels wide. Multi-column tables are often awkward there without responsive layout, so we ask the assistant to use short lists. That is a product choice, not a claim that tables can never work on small screens.</p><h3>Render formatting safely</h3><p>An assistant may return Markdown such as <code>bold</code>. Leaving it untouched shows the punctuation. Some rendering approaches produce an HTML string and insert it with <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>; others render interface elements directly. Raw HTML insertion is not required by every Markdown library.</p><p>Treat model output as untrusted input. Visitors can influence it, including by asking the assistant to repeat material they supplied.</p><p>We use a small renderer for bold, italic, code and headings that returns UI elements rather than raw HTML. That removes the raw-HTML insertion path from this component. It does not prove the entire application is immune to XSS: links, URL protocols and extensions still need appropriate validation.</p><h3>Match the reader&#39;s language</h3><p>The site has Vietnamese and English versions. An originally fixed Vietnamese instruction caused English-page visitors to receive Vietnamese answers.</p><p>Instead of maintaining two diverging rule sets, keep the underlying rules in one language and add a response-language instruction:</p><pre><code>RESPONSE LANGUAGE — security and access rules still apply\\n- The brief below is written in Vietnamese. Follow its rules and answer in English.\\n- If the visitor requests another language, use that language.</code></pre><p>A language preference should never override access or security rules.</p><h2>Before enabling a business chatbot</h2><ol><li><p>List prohibited claims or disclosures: prices, delivery commitments, sensitive data.</p></li><li><p>Remove unnecessary sensitive information from context.</p></li><li><p>Put required facts behind authorized lookup tools.</p></li><li><p>Enforce retrieval and validate IDs, counts and permissions in the application.</p></li><li><p>Render safely; validate links as well as text.</p></li><li><p>Test ordinary and adversarial phrasings of the questions you most worry about.</p></li></ol><p>The six invented names were discovered through an ordinary question. Testing should include those, not just elaborate attacks.</p><hr><p><em>The six-person incident is retained from the October 2026 Zukatek draft. Its original incident log is not available here for independent verification.</em></p><p>Reference: <a href=\\"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm092025-misinformation/\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">OWASP — LLM Misinformation</a>.</p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '3493cf5d-80c9-5187-bc75-86ba0d724cae', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "Website security: seven practical starting checks", "keywords": "website security, rate limiting, reCAPTCHA, Argon2id, HTTPS, secrets", "description": "Review password storage, rate limits, failure behavior, backup secrets, public data and sensitive-access logs for a business website."}, "slug": "practical-website-security-baseline", "title": "A practical website security baseline: seven starting points", "excerpt": "Seven checks for a small business website, from password hashing to secret-access logs, with the limits of each check made explicit.", "body_html": "<figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 654\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"654\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Seven minimum security tasks in order\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"654\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Seven minimums — in the order to do them</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"106.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">1</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"94\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Hash passwords with argon2id</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"116\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Not MD5, not bare SHA-256</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"108\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">fail this and fix it first</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"180.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">2</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"168\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Rate-limit every public door</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"190\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">The one that spends money is the worst</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"182\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"254.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">3</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"242\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Answer 429, not 503</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"264\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Say what is actually happening</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"256\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"328.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">4</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"316\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Fail closed, never open</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"338\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">A check that cannot run must refuse</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"330\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">the most important rule here</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"364\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"364\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"364\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"402.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">5</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"390\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">No secrets in the source</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"412\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Check the database backups too</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"404\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"438\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"438\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"438\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"476.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">6</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"464\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">A permission level per kind of data</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"486\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">One choke point for every public read</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"478\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"512\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"512\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"512\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"550.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">7</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"538\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Log READING a secret as well</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"560\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Who, when, which key — never the value</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"552\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"610\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">This is a first pass, not a full audit: software updates, MFA, backups with a</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"630\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">restore test, HTTPS and session management still have to be covered.</text></svg></figure><p>This is a practical starting order for seven checks, not a complete security audit. Initial checks can be quick; establishing that a system is secure takes more work. Include software updates, MFA, backups and restore tests, HTTPS, session management and incident monitoring in the wider plan.</p><p>The examples below are retained from the project draft, including problems the team reported finding in its own system.</p><h2>1. Use a password hashing function</h2><p>If a database leaks, password storage affects how easily attackers can recover passwords and try them elsewhere.</p><ul><li><p>Bare MD5, SHA-1 or SHA-256 are unsuitable: their speed helps attackers.</p></li><li><p>bcrypt remains an option for existing systems with appropriate configuration.</p></li><li><p>OWASP recommends Argon2id for new systems; its memory cost raises the cost of guessing.</p></li></ul><p>Look at the stored format as a first check. Prefixes such as <code>$argon2id$</code> or <code>$2a$</code>/<code>$2b$</code> help identify the algorithm, but do not prove that its parameters, salt or implementation are adequate. A 32- or 64-character hexadecimal value deserves investigation, not an automatic diagnosis from appearance alone.</p><h2>2. Rate-limit public entry points</h2><p>Login, contact forms, newsletter subscriptions and chat can all be called repeatedly. Pay particular attention to endpoints that trigger paid services, such as an AI provider.</p><p>The draft reports adding nginx limits and observing:</p><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Test</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Reported result</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Normal page visit with assets</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>No blocked requests</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>150 sequential requests</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>17 blocked</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>300 parallel requests</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>225 blocked</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Testing normal traffic matters as much as blocking abusive traffic. A rule that blocks real customers is likely to be removed, leaving no protection.</p><p>Test against the configured threshold on staging or another authorized system. Not seeing a <code>429</code> after 100 calls does not prove a limiter is absent: the threshold, time window, burst allowance and identity key all matter. Avoid unplanned load on production or paid downstream services.</p><h2>3. Use a meaningful status code</h2><p><code>429</code> says the client is sending too many requests; <code>503</code> indicates service unavailability. Use the response that describes the situation.</p><p>Automatic blocking tools still need matching log rules. They do not universally understand every <code>429</code> or ignore every <code>503</code> without configuration.</p><h2>4. Define failure behavior explicitly</h2><p>When an action requires a security check, a failed check must not silently count as a successful one.</p><p>For example, a reCAPTCHA verification request that cannot reach Google should return an error rather than mark the submission valid:</p><pre><code>// Google unreachable is NOT a pass.\\nreturn fmt.Errorf(&#34;recaptcha unreachable: %w&#34;, err)</code></pre><p>That can interrupt a legitimate contact request. Design any fallback channel deliberately according to the risk of the workflow.</p><p>Missing keys are a configuration error. An admin interface should refuse to enable required CAPTCHA until keys are present and clearly show its state. If verification is required at runtime but cannot be performed, reject the action or use an explicitly designed alternative. Do not silently skip it while presenting the feature as protected.</p><h2>5. Review secrets in backups too</h2><p>Secrets can appear outside source code. The draft describes finding a reCAPTCHA secret in a binary column called <code>value_encrypted</code> after an earlier scan missed it.</p><p>It identifies these project-specific locations:</p><pre><code>ai_providers.api_key\\npush_keys.private_key\\nsystem_settings.value_encrypted\\ntwo_factor_secrets.secret</code></pre><p>Searching for <code>sk-</code>, <code>api_key</code>, <code>secret</code> or <code>BEGIN PRIVATE KEY</code> is only a first screen. Secrets may be in text, JSON, binary or encrypted fields. Review the schema and configuration, restrict backup access, encrypt backups and set retention limits. If a secret was exposed, revoke or rotate it.</p><h2>6. Limit public data deliberately</h2><p>Giving every account access to everything turns one compromised account into a much wider incident.</p><p>The draft reports a public team endpoint returning internal names and email addresses that were also login identifiers. That increases opportunities for account enumeration and phishing; an email address alone is not a password.</p><p>Use a central public-response projection that exposes only approved fields. Check that every new endpoint uses it; an architectural description is not proof that no bypass exists.</p><p>Inspect public HTML and APIs, then ask whether each disclosed field is actually needed by visitors.</p><h2>7. Log sensitive reads</h2><p>Changes leave traces more readily than viewing an existing API key. If a key is later misused, it helps to know who requested access to it.</p><p>The project draft describes three controls for viewing a stored key:</p><ol><li><p>Appropriate administrator permission.</p></li><li><p>Reauthentication to reduce risk from an unattended session; consider MFA for sensitive operations.</p></li><li><p>Rate limiting so the reauthentication endpoint does not become a password-guessing route.</p></li></ol><p>Record the actor, time and key identifier—not the secret value. Prefer masking and rotation over revealing a recoverable key whenever the workflow allows it.</p><h2>Initial review checklist</h2><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Area</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>First check</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Passwords</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Identify the algorithm and review its configuration</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Rate limits</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Test configured thresholds in an authorized environment</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Error codes</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Check the response for a throttled request</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Verification failure</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Confirm failure does not silently pass</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Backups</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Review secret locations and access controls</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Public data</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Inspect actual public responses</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Secret reads</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Confirm access is authorized and logged without secret values</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Prioritize dangerous password storage promptly. If one check fails open, review other error paths too; the same handling pattern may have been reused.</p><hr><p><em>Situations and request counts are retained from the October 2026 Zukatek draft. The original logs were not available for independent verification.</em></p><p>Reference: <a href=\\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a>.</p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '3e9fc822-291a-5a63-a145-33d6191ccd59', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "Technology in 2026: what small businesses should do first", "keywords": "technology 2026, AI agents, small business, digital transformation", "description": "Practical priorities for small businesses: reliable data, measured infrastructure improvements, and controlled AI automation."}, "slug": "technology-priorities-small-businesses-2026", "title": "Technology priorities for small businesses in 2026", "excerpt": "Three changes that affect decisions in a company of 5–50 people, what to do first, and where the promises need closer scrutiny.", "body_html": "<p>Every December, the internet fills up with lists of next year&#39;s technology trends. Many are plausible but unhelpful: they describe a direction without telling you what to do on Monday morning.</p><p>This article takes a different approach. It focuses on changes that affect decisions in a company of 5–50 people, and suggests an order for addressing them.</p><h2>1. AI is moving from answering questions to taking action</h2><p>The significant change is not just smarter models. It is giving them permission to act.</p><p>A chatbot that answers a question is one thing. A system that looks up orders, creates contact records, or schedules appointments has a different risk profile and a different kind of usefulness.</p><p>You do not need an unverified adoption forecast to decide whether to use an agent. Evaluate the particular task, the permissions involved, and the damage an incorrect action could cause.</p><p>Once AI can act, its mistakes can become errors in your business records rather than a sentence someone reads and ignores.</p><p>This leads to a design principle from building our own assistant: <strong>control AI through architecture as well as instructions</strong>. If an assistant needs employee information, give it a controlled lookup tool. However, that alone does not stop it inventing names. The application must validate names and counts against the tool result, or render the approved records directly using a fixed template.</p><p>We discuss this in <a href=\\"/en/blog/reducing-chatbot-fabrications\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">the article on reducing fabricated chatbot answers</a>.</p><h3>What to do</h3><p>Start with a repetitive task that has clear rules and limited consequences if it goes wrong: summarizing material, classifying email, or drafting replies. After a sustained period of reliable operation—three months is a useful review point—consider whether it should receive write access.</p><h2>2. Measure infrastructure before buying more of it</h2><p>Five years ago, “we are too small to build our own software” could be a reasonable objection. Infrastructure costs alone are a less convincing explanation today.</p><p>The original project draft describes a public server-rendered website, an admin interface, an API, a database, and an AI assistant on a virtual server with four CPU cores and 2.9 GB of RAM. It records a load average of 0.06 at the time of measurement. That is an observation, not a capacity test.</p><p>The more useful point is where to look for a bottleneck. The draft records an internal response around 3 ms and an external request taking 100–130 ms. That suggests measuring the network and asset loading separately. Those different measurements do not establish that exactly 97% of the delay has one cause.</p><p>There is <strong>no basis for buying more CPU from these measurements alone</strong>, unless other evidence shows that CPU is the constraint.</p><h3>What to do</h3><p>Measure before upgrading. <a href=\\"/en/blog/server-optimization-measure-first\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">Server optimization: measure first</a> explains the process and the limitations of the recorded figures.</p><h2>3. Reliable data matters more than another tool</h2><p>This is the least exciting part, and often the most important.</p><p>The useful tasks above depend on information being accurate, complete enough, and retrievable. If customer records are scattered across three spreadsheets, two mailboxes, and a notebook, AI will struggle to produce dependable answers. It may help classify the material, but people still need to check it and agree on a consistent source of record.</p><p>My suggested order is:</p><ol><li><p>Bring the data into a place where it can be queried.</p></li><li><p>Digitize recurring transactions: orders, contact requests, appointments.</p></li><li><p>Introduce AI for reading and summarizing.</p></li><li><p>Only then consider allowing it to write.</p></li></ol><p>Skipping directly from scattered data to autonomous actions is an expensive way to discover that the foundations were missing.</p><h2>A promise to examine carefully: “no-code replaces developers”</h2><p>Low-code and no-code platforms can be useful for forms, approval workflows, and dashboards. These are reasonable places to use them.</p><p>But a polished demonstration does not show every operational responsibility. The original project account describes problems such as certificate issuance, an AI provider running out of quota, incompatible PostgreSQL versions, and a CDN holding an outdated service worker.</p><p>These are examples of issues a no-code demonstration may not cover. They do not establish that every no-code platform is incapable of handling them.</p><p>Use such platforms for internal workflows where recovery is straightforward. For customer-facing features or payments, first examine access control, integration support, backups, and incident handling.</p><figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 432\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"432\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Four steps in priority order\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"432\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">The order that works — skipping costs money</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"106.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">1</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"94\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Collect the data</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"116\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">One place, queryable</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"108\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">everything else rests on it</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"180.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">2</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"168\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Digitise transactions</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"190\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Orders, enquiries, bookings</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"182\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">start with repeated work</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"254.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">3</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"242\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">AI reads and summarises</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"264\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">No write access yet</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"256\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">a mistake harms nobody</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"328.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">4</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"316\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">AI is allowed to act</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"338\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">It can write data</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"330\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">a mistake lands in real data</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"388\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Jumping from step 1 to step 4 is the fastest way to waste money: an agent</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"408\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">acting on dirty data only produces dirty data faster — and for real.</text></svg></figure><h2>Where to start next quarter</h2><p>If you can do only one thing, start by making your data consistent and retrievable. It may not make an exciting meeting presentation, but it supports everything else in this article. New tools still need someone to decide what the records mean and who is responsible for them.</p><hr><p><em>Infrastructure figures are retained from the original Zukatek draft. This review had neither the original logs nor server access to verify them independently.</em></p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '5eb8c4fb-5989-51c1-b18a-635d80045a77', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "Customer inquiry notifications: Web Push, email or Zalo", "keywords": "Web Push, email notifications, Zalo, team notifications", "description": "Choose notification channels with delivery limits, permissions, deduplication, ownership and fallback in mind."}, "slug": "team-notifications-web-push-email-zalo", "title": "Notifying the team about a customer: Web Push, email or Zalo?", "excerpt": "Choose an internal notification channel around devices, responsibility and failed delivery. Push and email complement each other; verify Zalo policy before integrating.", "body_html": "<p>The task sounds simple: <strong>a customer leaves an inquiry on the website; notify the person responsible for handling it</strong>. But successful submission to a notification service does not mean an employee has seen the message.</p><h2>Three channels, three roles</h2><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Criterion</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Web Push</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Email</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Official Zalo integration</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Useful role</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Alert people who enabled notifications</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Record and supplementary channel</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Reach a Zalo-based team when the provider supports the workflow</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Requirements</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>HTTPS, service worker, permission, compatible device</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Recipient address and sending configuration</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Suitable API product, account, permissions and templates</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Delivery</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Can be delayed or suppressed by the OS</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Can be delayed, rejected or sent to spam</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Depends on policy, account and service state</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Cost</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>May have no per-message fee; operation still costs money</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Depends on infrastructure or sending plan</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Confirm current pricing and conditions</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Durable record</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Store it in the application</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Usually retained as mail</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Depends on the integration product</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Do not call Zalo the worst option based on an indirectly quoted price or sending window. Distinguish OA chat from template messaging services: they are not one API with one set of rules.</p><h2>What to confirm before choosing Zalo</h2><p>The earlier draft included prices, sending hours, conversation windows and token lifetimes without sufficient primary policy evidence. This edition removes those figures rather than using them to reject a channel.</p><p>Before designing the integration, consult <a href=\\"https://developers.zalo.me/\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">Zalo Developers</a> and confirm with the provider whether employee notifications are supported, what recipient consent is required, whether templates need approval, and what sending limits, charges and reauthorization procedures apply. Do not build around circumventing an API&#39;s intended use.</p><p>Store tokens securely, handle expiry and revoked permissions, prevent concurrent refreshes if that token mechanism requires it, and alert on broken connections. Follow the documentation for the specific API product in use.</p><h2>Web Push suits teams that work in a browser</h2><p>Web Push can receive messages when a page is not in the foreground or is not currently loaded. It needs an active service worker and user permission. Visible delivery also depends on the browser, operating system, network, battery settings and do-not-disturb mode. Test on actual devices; do not promise instant delivery.</p><p>A commercial messaging intermediary is not mandatory, but delivery still depends on the browser ecosystem&#39;s push service. VAPID keys are only part of the implementation: manage subscriptions, encrypt payloads and handle expired endpoints too.</p><figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 352\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"352\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Comparison of three internal alert channels\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"352\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Three internal alert channels — one role each</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"230\\" height=\\"212\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><path d=\\"M32 76 a8 8 0 0 1 8 -8 h214 a8 8 0 0 1 8 8 v26 h-230 z\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></path><text x=\\"46\\" y=\\"92\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Web Push</text><text x=\\"250\\" y=\\"91\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">THE NUDGE</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"124\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"128\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Needs HTTPS and a live</text><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"143\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">service worker</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"158\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"162\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Each person enables it per</text><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"177\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">browser</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"192\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"196\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">The operating system may</text><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"211\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">delay or block it</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"226\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"230\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">No promise of instant — test</text><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"245\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">on real devices</text><rect x=\\"284\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"230\\" height=\\"212\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><path d=\\"M284 76 a8 8 0 0 1 8 -8 h214 a8 8 0 0 1 8 8 v26 h-230 z\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></path><text x=\\"298\\" y=\\"92\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Email</text><text x=\\"502\\" y=\\"91\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">THE RECORD</text><circle cx=\\"300\\" cy=\\"124\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></circle><text x=\\"312\\" y=\\"128\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">An address is all it takes</text><text x=\\"312\\" y=\\"143\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">to send</text><circle cx=\\"300\\" cy=\\"158\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></circle><text x=\\"312\\" y=\\"162\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">May be delayed,</text><text x=\\"312\\" y=\\"177\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">spam-filtered or refused</text><circle cx=\\"300\\" cy=\\"192\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#2563eb\\"></circle><text x=\\"312\\" y=\\"196\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Leaves a copy that can be</text><text x=\\"312\\" y=\\"211\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">looked up later</text><rect x=\\"536\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"230\\" height=\\"212\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><path d=\\"M536 76 a8 8 0 0 1 8 -8 h214 a8 8 0 0 1 8 8 v26 h-230 z\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></path><text x=\\"550\\" y=\\"92\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Zalo</text><text x=\\"754\\" y=\\"91\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">CONFIRM FIRST</text><circle cx=\\"552\\" cy=\\"124\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></circle><text x=\\"564\\" y=\\"128\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Whether staff alerts are a</text><text x=\\"564\\" y=\\"143\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">supported use</text><circle cx=\\"552\\" cy=\\"158\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></circle><text x=\\"564\\" y=\\"162\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Recipient conditions,</text><text x=\\"564\\" y=\\"177\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">template review, send limits</text><circle cx=\\"552\\" cy=\\"192\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></circle><text x=\\"564\\" y=\\"196\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Cost per the quote current</text><text x=\\"564\\" y=\\"211\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">at the time</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"308\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">This diagram does not confirm Zalo pricing or policy: check the documentation</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"328\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">for the exact API product before designing an integration.</text></svg></figure><h2>Four design decisions</h2><h3>1. Keep the payload small and fetch details after authentication</h3><p>A notification can appear on a lock screen. Send an event type and an opaque identifier rather than the customer&#39;s message or personal information. On click, check the session and authorization before fetching details. An identifier does not replace access control; do not put access tokens or secret keys in the notification link.</p><h3>2. Group related events</h3><p>Five lines from one customer do not necessarily need five alerts. Notify on the first message or group events within a short window. Repeated newsletter subscriptions should not count as new customers simply because the endpoint returns success again.</p><h3>3. Route to an owner and provide a backup</h3><p>Route according to responsibility and permission to view the record. Do not alert people who cannot open the destination screen. Escalate to the backup person if nobody accepts responsibility within the agreed time.</p><p>Track “event stored,” “submitted to service” and “accepted for handling” separately. Do not collapse them into a misleading single “delivered” state.</p><h3>4. Respect working hours</h3><p>Use persistent notifications only when supported and appropriate. Define on-call coverage, quiet hours and priority. An inquiry at 11 p.m. is not automatically a reason to wake the entire team.</p><h2>An old service worker can discard new notifications</h2><p>The draft describes an older worker ignoring new message types:</p><pre><code>if (data.kind &amp;&amp; data.kind !== &#34;visitor_waiting&#34;) return;</code></pre><p>A CDN can retain an old file; the worker installation and activation lifecycle can also delay deployment. Inspect both instead of only clearing browser cache.</p><p>For a stable worker URL, use a suitable revalidation policy such as <code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code>. Check CDN overrides and whether a new worker is waiting to activate. <code>no-cache</code> does not prohibit storage. Add a version identifier so you can tell which worker a device is running, then test the complete flow on real devices.</p><h2>A starting choice for a small team</h2><p>Start with <strong>Web Push for alerts and email for a record</strong> if the team already uses the admin interface regularly. Design a queue, bounded retries, deduplication and failure alerts. Keep an in-application list of unresolved inquiries so work does not depend entirely on a notification sound.</p><p>If the team mainly uses Zalo, evaluate its official integration after confirming the supported workflow and current policy. The final criterion is whether someone takes responsibility within the agreed time, not which channel feels most familiar.</p><hr><p><em>The worker incident is retained from the October 2026 Zukatek draft; its original logs are unavailable for independent verification. This article does not certify Zalo pricing or policy.</em></p><p>References: <a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Push_API\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">MDN Push API</a>, <a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Cache-Control\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">MDN Cache-Control</a>.</p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '9be1238e-8722-55e9-84e5-26be05ec9e73', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "When to use a CDN: caching, measurement and operational tradeoffs", "keywords": "CDN, Cloudflare, TTFB, edge cache, service worker", "description": "How to compare direct and CDN routes fairly, interpret REVALIDATED, protect private responses and manage service worker updates."}, "slug": "when-a-cdn-helps", "title": "When a CDN helps—and what a quick test cannot tell you", "excerpt": "Turning a CDN off and on taught us more about measurement and cache configuration than a simple faster-or-slower verdict.", "body_html": "<p>The project draft describes turning a CDN off, then enabling it again a few days later. The useful lesson is not that either configuration always wins. It is how easily an incomplete test can support an overly confident conclusion.</p><h2>Why the proxy was disabled</h2><p>The page felt slow. An early test recorded 348–838 ms through the CDN against an internal response around 3 ms. But the test ran from the origin server, out through the CDN and back to itself. That is not the route a user takes.</p><p>A later test from a machine in Vietnam recorded:</p><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Route</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>TLS timing</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>TTFB</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Total</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>CDN proxy</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.13–0.16 s</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.31–0.49 s</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.32–0.50 s</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Direct</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.06–0.08 s</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.08–0.10 s</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.10–0.13 s</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>This is not a complete controlled comparison.</strong> The routes tested different applications on the same server. TTFB includes their different processing work.</p><p>The TLS column is not conclusive either. We need to know whether it represents the TLS phase alone or a cumulative <code>curl</code> timestamp, whether connections were reused, and which protocols were negotiated. The table does not establish a fixed 70–80 ms CDN penalty.</p><p>An unfavorable route to the edge is one possible explanation, but identifying that requires routing evidence and the edge location code. Latency alone does not establish the location.</p><p>The team disabled the proxy at the time. These figures alone do not prove that was the better performance choice.</p><h2>Why it was enabled again</h2><p>The draft later records these ranges for the same page:</p><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Site</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Before, direct</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>After, CDN with caching</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p><code>zukatek.com</code></p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.10–0.13 s</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>0.11–0.13 s</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The ranges overlap in the samples recorded. An accompanying header was:</p><pre><code>cf-cache-status: REVALIDATED</code></pre><p>This means the cached resource was validated with the origin before being served. It does not prove there was no origin request or that the edge was in Vietnam. Headers, logs and controlled repeats are needed to explain the result.</p><p>A CDN may also provide DDoS mitigation and reduce direct origin exposure when DNS and firewall rules are configured correctly. It does not guarantee the origin IP has never been exposed.</p><p>The project account also describes the CDN accepting port 80 traffic while some direct international connections could not reach the origin. That is a separate operational observation, not proof of a universal CDN advantage.</p><h2>Ask what can be cached</h2><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Content</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Potential benefit</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Important condition</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Images, CSS, JavaScript</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Often substantial</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Long caching needs versioned or hashed URLs and no private content</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Public pages that change infrequently</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Often useful</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Choose freshness and invalidation rules deliberately</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Authenticated pages</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Depends</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Usually bypass shared caching; routing and protection may still help</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Write APIs</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Depends</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Do not cache responses casually; protection may still be useful</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Origin distance and cache hit rate matter. For dynamic content, include routing, connection management and protection in the evaluation rather than assuming the proxy is only an added cost.</p><figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 352\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"352\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"When a CDN helps and when it needs thought\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"352\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Where a CDN helps — ask whether the content can be cached</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"357\\" height=\\"212\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><path d=\\"M32 76 a8 8 0 0 1 8 -8 h341 a8 8 0 0 1 8 8 v26 h-357 z\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></path><text x=\\"46\\" y=\\"92\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">CLEARLY HELPS</text><text x=\\"377\\" y=\\"91\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">CACHEABLE</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"124\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"128\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Images, CSS and JavaScript with a version hash</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"158\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"162\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Public pages that rarely change</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"192\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"196\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">A server far from its readers</text><circle cx=\\"48\\" cy=\\"226\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#15803d\\"></circle><text x=\\"60\\" y=\\"230\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Bandwidth-level DDoS protection</text><rect x=\\"411\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"357\\" height=\\"212\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><path d=\\"M411 76 a8 8 0 0 1 8 -8 h341 a8 8 0 0 1 8 8 v26 h-357 z\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></path><text x=\\"425\\" y=\\"92\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"14.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">NEEDS THOUGHT</text><text x=\\"756\\" y=\\"91\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"10.5\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">IT DEPENDS</text><circle cx=\\"427\\" cy=\\"124\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></circle><text x=\\"439\\" y=\\"128\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Signed-in pages usually bypass a shared cache</text><circle cx=\\"427\\" cy=\\"158\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></circle><text x=\\"439\\" y=\\"162\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Write APIs must not be cached casually</text><circle cx=\\"427\\" cy=\\"192\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></circle><text x=\\"439\\" y=\\"196\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">A server already close to its readers</text><circle cx=\\"427\\" cy=\\"226\\" r=\\"3\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></circle><text x=\\"439\\" y=\\"230\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Content that differs per person</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"308\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Measured from Vietnam against the same server: direct 0.10–0.13 s · through</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"328\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">the CDN uncached 0.32–0.50 s · through the CDN cached 0.11–0.13 s.</text></svg></figure><h2>Three operational costs to plan for</h2><h3>1. Client IP handling changes</h3><p>The origin sees connections from the proxy. A rate limiter can accidentally treat unrelated visitors as one client if it keys only on that address.</p><p>Trust forwarded client-IP headers only from authenticated, trusted proxies. Restrict origin traffic appropriately and configure the proxy chain. Accepting an IP header from arbitrary clients permits spoofing.</p><p>The draft also describes shared logs for a proxied and an unproxied application. An automatic ban based on those logs could block a CDN address range. Separate the logs and review the ban rules before enabling enforcement.</p><h3>2. An old control file can delay an update</h3><p>The draft reports an outdated service worker with a cache age of 6,411 seconds. The old worker silently discarded newly introduced notification kinds.</p><p>For stable worker URLs and runtime configuration, use an appropriate revalidation policy such as <code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code>. Check CDN overrides and the worker&#39;s installation and activation lifecycle too. <code>no-cache</code> permits storage but requires validation before reuse; <code>no-store</code> prohibits storage. Hashed application assets can still use long caching.</p><h3>3. Origin certificates have a specific trust scope</h3><p>Some CDNs supply origin certificates trusted by that CDN rather than ordinary browsers. Disabling the proxy can then expose a certificate browsers reject.</p><p>The project used a publicly trusted Let&#39;s Encrypt certificate at the origin to support both configurations. Check the trust scope of your own certificate before changing the route.</p><h2>How to decide</h2><ol><li><p>Test the same content from real user locations, direct and proxied.</p></li><li><p>Record cache state, protocol and connection reuse.</p></li><li><p>Estimate how much traffic can safely use shared caching.</p></li><li><p>Include protection and operational requirements in the decision.</p></li><li><p>Repeat after deployment rather than relying on a provider&#39;s promise.</p></li></ol><hr><p><em>Figures are retained from the October 2026 Zukatek draft and were not independently verified. The first table compares different applications and must not be used as a CDN benchmark.</em></p><p>References: <a href=\\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/concepts/cache-responses/\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">Cloudflare cache responses</a>, <a href=\\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Cache-Control\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">MDN Cache-Control</a>.</p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO translations (entity, entity_id, locale, values, updated_at)
VALUES ('article', '9d3b6d45-dec8-5495-b0be-674518abfdd3', 'en', E'{"seo": {"title": "Seven PostgreSQL backup and migration traps", "keywords": "PostgreSQL, pg_dump, pg_restore, backup, migration, restore", "description": "Practical checks for PostgreSQL version compatibility, backup secrets, foreign keys, migrations, file permissions and safe test-data cleanup."}, "slug": "seven-database-migration-traps", "title": "Seven traps when backing up and moving a database", "excerpt": "A backup that restores on a development machine may still fail at the destination. Check versions, secrets, constraints, files and cleanup scope.", "body_html": "<p>The original project account describes rebuilding a production database from a backup and encountering seven problems that had not appeared in local tests. The useful part is the sequence of checks those failures suggest.</p><h2>1. PostgreSQL versions differ</h2><p>The draft places the development server on PostgreSQL major version 18 and the destination on major version 16. No <code>SELECT version()</code> output is available here to confirm the patch versions originally claimed.</p><p>A dump produced by newer tooling can include settings an older destination does not understand, for example:</p><pre><code>SET transaction_timeout = 0;</code></pre><p>PostgreSQL 16 does not recognize that setting. With <code>ON_ERROR_STOP=1</code>, restoring stops at the error. Removing one line does not establish that the rest of a newer dump is compatible with an older server.</p><p>Record the source server, destination server, dump tool and restore tool versions. <code>pg_dump</code> cannot read a server newer than its own major version, and restoring into an older major version is not guaranteed. Prefer a destination on the same or a newer major, or design and test a compatible export/import path.</p><p>Before touching the real destination, restore into a scratch database there:</p><pre><code>createdb db_scratch &amp;&amp; psql -d db_scratch -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f ddl.sql -f data.sql</code></pre><h2>2. Secrets live in unexpected columns</h2><p>The first scan in the account examined an ordinary settings field and found nothing. A later scan found a reCAPTCHA secret in <code>value_encrypted</code>.</p><p>The project draft identifies four binary fields holding sensitive material:</p><pre><code>ai_providers.api_key              encrypted provider API key\\npush_keys.private_key            push private key\\nsystem_settings.value_encrypted  reCAPTCHA secret\\ntwo_factor_secrets.secret        per-user 2FA secret</code></pre><p>A dump intended for source control must not carry live secrets. Removing a file later does not remove its earlier Git history.</p><p>Inspect the schema rather than only searching familiar column names:</p><pre><code>SELECT table_name||&#39;.&#39;||column_name FROM information_schema.columns\\nWHERE data_type = &#39;bytea&#39; AND table_schema = &#39;public&#39;;</code></pre><p>This query covers binary columns in <code>public</code>, not every possible secret. Text, JSON, other schemas and external configuration also need review.</p><h2>3. Data loading and foreign keys need a plan</h2><p>A data-only restore into tables with existing foreign keys can fail if rows arrive before their referenced parents. A partial restore can leave a database in an incomplete state.</p><p>For a fresh destination, use a suitable full archive and restore schema, data, then post-data objects such as indexes and constraints. Custom format alone does not solve every data-only restore into an already constrained schema.</p><p>Consider <code>--disable-triggers</code> only in a controlled procedure with sufficient privileges and subsequent integrity checks. It is not a default way to bypass data correctness.</p><h2>4. “Restore completed” is not the same as “correct”</h2><p>A dump can restore without errors yet still mismatch the application release. The project used its migration command as one check:</p><pre><code>go run ./cmd/api migrate\\n→ &#34;no migrations to run. current version: 51&#34;</code></pre><p>Compare migration state with the deployment plan. Pending migrations may be normal during an upgrade. No pending migrations does not prove the data is correct either: check record counts, constraints and important application flows.</p><p>Also avoid reading the wrong exit status from a pipeline:</p><pre><code>psql ... | tail -2 ; echo $?</code></pre><p>In Bash, that reports <code>tail</code>&#39;s status, which can be zero even when <code>psql</code> failed. Use <code>set -o pipefail</code>, read <code>${PIPESTATUS[0]}</code> immediately after the pipeline, or remove the pipeline. These are Bash conventions, not interchangeable PowerShell syntax.</p><h2>5. Distinguish a recovery backup from a seed dataset</h2><p>A full database includes sessions, logs, conversations and customer information. Those may not belong in a seed dataset for a new environment.</p><p>But do not mechanically remove operational data from a disaster-recovery backup: some of it may be necessary for recovery or investigation.</p><table><tbody><tr><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>A seed often keeps</p></th><th colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>A seed may exclude; a recovery backup needs its own policy</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Approved content, configuration, users and permissions</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Activity logs</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Translations, menus and categories</p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Login sessions</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p></p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Chat conversations</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p></p></td><td colspan=\\"1\\" rowspan=\\"1\\"><p>Test customer data</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Choose the purpose before choosing what to exclude. Protect any personal data that must be retained.</p><h2>6. Database records do not include files on disk</h2><p>A row in a <code>files</code> table can point to an image without containing the actual image. Copy filesystem assets separately and check that they match the records.</p><p>The draft describes an upload failure after directory creation:</p><pre><code>install -d -o zukatek -g zukatek /var/lib/.../members/&lt;id&gt;/</code></pre><p>Specifying ownership here is not a reason to assume all parent directories have the required permissions. Test as the application account:</p><pre><code>su -s /bin/sh zukatek -c &#39;mkdir -p /path/to/_probe &amp;&amp; echo ok&#39;</code></pre><p>Use a designated probe location. Verify traversal, reading and actual writing rather than inferring access from an ownership listing.</p><h2>7. Cleanup can affect more than the named tables</h2><p>The draft describes this test cleanup:</p><pre><code>TRUNCATE ai_messages, ai_conversations CASCADE;</code></pre><p>It reports that the cascade also affected <code>leads</code> and <code>lead_products</code>, which were fortunately empty at the time.</p><p>A filtered delete is narrower, for example:</p><pre><code>DELETE FROM leads WHERE email LIKE &#39;%@test.local&#39;;</code></pre><p>But even a condition can match the wrong records, and <code>DELETE</code> can invoke cascades or triggers. Prefer explicit test IDs or batch markers, preview the scope with <code>SELECT</code>, use a transaction, and inspect affected rows before commit.</p><figure class=\\"kd-svg\\"><svg xmlns=\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\" viewBox=\\"0 0 800 802\\" width=\\"800\\" height=\\"802\\" role=\\"img\\" aria-label=\\"Nine steps of a data migration in order\\"><rect width=\\"800\\" height=\\"802\\" rx=\\"10\\" fill=\\"#f7f9fc\\"></rect><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"42\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"700\\">Moving data — the two amber steps are the ones skipped most</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"68\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"106.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">1</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"94\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Record both versions</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"116\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Source and destination</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"108\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"142\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"180.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">2</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"168\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">List the binary columns</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"190\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Which ones hold secrets</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"182\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"216\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"254.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">3</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"242\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Separate operational data</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"264\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Under a stated policy</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"256\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"290\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"328.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">4</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"316\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Dump, then scan for secrets</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"338\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Several patterns, not one</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"330\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"364\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"364\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"364\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"402.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">5</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"390\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">BACK UP the destination</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"412\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Before touching it</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"404\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">the step that saves you</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"438\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"438\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"438\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"476.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">6</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"464\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Restore into a scratch database there</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"486\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Same server, same version</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"478\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#b45309\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\">the step that saves you</text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"512\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"512\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"512\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"550.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">7</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"538\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Run the migrations</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"560\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">They must come out clean</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"552\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"586\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"586\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"586\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"624.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">8</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"612\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Load for real</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"634\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Then reconcile the row counts</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"626\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"660\\" width=\\"736\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" stroke=\\"#dbe3ee\\"></rect><rect x=\\"32\\" y=\\"660\\" width=\\"46\\" height=\\"64\\" rx=\\"8\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><rect x=\\"66\\" y=\\"660\\" width=\\"12\\" height=\\"64\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\"></rect><text x=\\"55\\" y=\\"698.0\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"17\\" fill=\\"#ffffff\\" font-weight=\\"700\\" text-anchor=\\"middle\\">9</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"686\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"15\\" fill=\\"#16202e\\" font-weight=\\"600\\">Copy the files, then TEST A WRITE</text><text x=\\"94\\" y=\\"708\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">As the application&#39;s own account</text><text x=\\"752\\" y=\\"700\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#64748b\\" font-weight=\\"600\\" text-anchor=\\"end\\"></text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"758\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">Clear test data with a conditional DELETE. TRUNCATE … CASCADE takes more</text><text x=\\"32\\" y=\\"778\\" font-family=\\"system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif\\" font-size=\\"12.5\\" fill=\\"#5b6b80\\">than you think.</text></svg></figure><h2>A compact procedure</h2><ol><li><p>Record server and tool versions.</p></li><li><p>Identify sensitive fields and decide what the export should contain.</p></li><li><p>Choose recovery backup or seed, with an explicit exclusion policy.</p></li><li><p>Export and review for secrets.</p></li><li><p>Back up the destination.</p></li><li><p>Rehearse the restore in a scratch database at the destination.</p></li><li><p>Check migrations, data and important application flows against the release.</p></li><li><p>Perform the planned production restore.</p></li><li><p>Copy filesystem assets and test with the application account.</p></li><li><p>Clean test records by verified identifiers, not blanket truncation.</p></li></ol><p>The destination backup and rehearsal are easy to skip and especially valuable when something unexpected happens.</p><hr><p><em>Incidents are retained from the October 2026 Zukatek draft; original logs are not available here for independent verification.</em></p><p>References: <a href=\\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/app-pgdump.html\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">PostgreSQL pg_dump</a>, <a href=\\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/app-pgrestore.html\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">PostgreSQL pg_restore</a>.</p>"}', '2026-10-18 13:30:33.129082+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, entity_id, locale) DO UPDATE SET
  values = EXCLUDED.values, updated_at = now();

