INSERT INTO published_docs (entity, locale, slug, entity_id, is_current, search_text, body, published_at, written_at)
VALUES ('article', 'en', 'seven-database-migration-traps', '9d3b6d45-dec8-5495-b0be-674518abfdd3', true, 'Seven traps when backing up and moving a database A backup that restores on a development machine may still fail at the destination. Check versions, secrets, constraints, files and cleanup scope.', E'{"seo": {"title": "Seven PostgreSQL backup and migration traps", "noindex": false, "keywords": "PostgreSQL, pg_dump, pg_restore, backup, migration, restore", "nofollow": false, "og_title": "", "description": "Practical checks for PostgreSQL version compatibility, backup secrets, foreign keys, migrations, file permissions and safe test-data cleanup.", "canonical_url": "", "og_description": ""}, "slug": "seven-database-migration-traps", "title": "Seven traps when backing up and moving a database", "excerpt": "A backup that restores on a development machine may still fail at the destination. Check versions, secrets, constraints, files and cleanup scope.", "og_path": null, "tag_ids": [], "body_html": "<p>The original project account describes rebuilding a production database from a backup and encountering seven problems that had not appeared in local tests. The useful part is the sequence of checks those failures suggest.</p>\\n<h2>1. PostgreSQL versions differ</h2>\\n<p>The draft places the development server on PostgreSQL major version 18 and the destination on major version 16. No <code>SELECT version()</code> output is available here to confirm the patch versions originally claimed.</p>\\n<p>A dump produced by newer tooling can include settings an older destination does not understand, for example:</p>\\n<pre><code>SET transaction_timeout = 0;</code></pre>\\n<p>PostgreSQL 16 does not recognize that setting. With <code>ON_ERROR_STOP=1</code>, restoring stops at the error. Removing one line does not establish that the rest of a newer dump is compatible with an older server.</p>\\n<p>Record the source server, destination server, dump tool and restore tool versions. <code>pg_dump</code> cannot read a server newer than its own major version, and restoring into an older major version is not guaranteed. Prefer a destination on the same or a newer major, or design and test a compatible export/import path.</p>\\n<p>Before touching the real destination, restore into a scratch database there:</p>\\n<pre><code>createdb db_scratch &amp;&amp; psql -d db_scratch -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f ddl.sql -f data.sql</code></pre>\\n<h2>2. Secrets live in unexpected columns</h2>\\n<p>The first scan in the account examined an ordinary settings field and found nothing. A later scan found a reCAPTCHA secret in <code>value_encrypted</code>.</p>\\n<p>The project draft identifies four binary fields holding sensitive material:</p>\\n<pre><code>ai_providers.api_key              encrypted provider API key\\npush_keys.private_key            push private key\\nsystem_settings.value_encrypted  reCAPTCHA secret\\ntwo_factor_secrets.secret        per-user 2FA secret</code></pre>\\n<p>A dump intended for source control must not carry live secrets. Removing a file later does not remove its earlier Git history.</p>\\n<p>Inspect the schema rather than only searching familiar column names:</p>\\n<pre><code>SELECT table_name||&#39;.&#39;||column_name FROM information_schema.columns\\nWHERE data_type = &#39;bytea&#39; AND table_schema = &#39;public&#39;;</code></pre>\\n<p>This query covers binary columns in <code>public</code>, not every possible secret. Text, JSON, other schemas and external configuration also need review.</p>\\n<h2>3. Data loading and foreign keys need a plan</h2>\\n<p>A data-only restore into tables with existing foreign keys can fail if rows arrive before their referenced parents. A partial restore can leave a database in an incomplete state.</p>\\n<p>For a fresh destination, use a suitable full archive and restore schema, data, then post-data objects such as indexes and constraints. Custom format alone does not solve every data-only restore into an already constrained schema.</p>\\n<p>Consider <code>--disable-triggers</code> only in a controlled procedure with sufficient privileges and subsequent integrity checks. It is not a default way to bypass data correctness.</p>\\n<h2>4. “Restore completed” is not the same as “correct”</h2>\\n<p>A dump can restore without errors yet still mismatch the application release. The project used its migration command as one check:</p>\\n<pre><code>go run ./cmd/api migrate\\n→ &#34;no migrations to run. current version: 51&#34;</code></pre>\\n<p>Compare migration state with the deployment plan. Pending migrations may be normal during an upgrade. No pending migrations does not prove the data is correct either: check record counts, constraints and important application flows.</p>\\n<p>Also avoid reading the wrong exit status from a pipeline:</p>\\n<pre><code>psql ... | tail -2 ; echo $?</code></pre>\\n<p>In Bash, that reports <code>tail</code>&#39;s status, which can be zero even when <code>psql</code> failed. Use <code>set -o pipefail</code>, read <code>${PIPESTATUS[0]}</code> immediately after the pipeline, or remove the pipeline. These are Bash conventions, not interchangeable PowerShell syntax.</p>\\n<h2>5. Distinguish a recovery backup from a seed dataset</h2>\\n<p>A full database includes sessions, logs, conversations and customer information. Those may not belong in a seed dataset for a new environment.</p>\\n<p>But do not mechanically remove operational data from a disaster-recovery backup: some of it may be necessary for recovery or investigation.</p>\\n<table><thead><tr><th>A seed often keeps</th><th>A seed may exclude; a recovery backup needs its own policy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Approved content, configuration, users and permissions</td><td>Activity logs</td></tr><tr><td>Translations, menus and categories</td><td>Login sessions</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Chat conversations</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Test customer data</td></tr></tbody></table>\\n<p>Choose the purpose before choosing what to exclude. Protect any personal data that must be retained.</p>\\n<h2>6. Database records do not include files on disk</h2>\\n<p>A row in a <code>files</code> table can point to an image without containing the actual image. Copy filesystem assets separately and check that they match the records.</p>\\n<p>The draft describes an upload failure after directory creation:</p>\\n<pre><code>install -d -o zukatek -g zukatek /var/lib/.../members/&lt;id&gt;/</code></pre>\\n<p>Specifying ownership here is not a reason to assume all parent directories have the required permissions. Test as the application account:</p>\\n<pre><code>su -s /bin/sh zukatek -c &#39;mkdir -p /path/to/_probe &amp;&amp; echo ok&#39;</code></pre>\\n<p>Use a designated probe location. Verify traversal, reading and actual writing rather than inferring access from an ownership listing.</p>\\n<h2>7. Cleanup can affect more than the named tables</h2>\\n<p>The draft describes this test cleanup:</p>\\n<pre><code>TRUNCATE ai_messages, ai_conversations CASCADE;</code></pre>\\n<p>It reports that the cascade also affected <code>leads</code> and <code>lead_products</code>, which were fortunately empty at the time.</p>\\n<p>A filtered delete is narrower, for example:</p>\\n<pre><code>DELETE FROM leads WHERE email LIKE &#39;%@test.local&#39;;</code></pre>\\n<p>But even a condition can match the wrong records, and <code>DELETE</code> can invoke cascades or triggers. Prefer explicit test IDs or batch markers, preview the scope with <code>SELECT</code>, use a transaction, and inspect affected rows before commit.</p>\\n<h2>A compact procedure</h2>\\n<ol><li>Record server and tool versions.</li><li>Identify sensitive fields and decide what the export should contain.</li><li>Choose recovery backup or seed, with an explicit exclusion policy.</li><li>Export and review for secrets.</li><li>Back up the destination.</li><li>Rehearse the restore in a scratch database at the destination.</li><li>Check migrations, data and important application flows against the release.</li><li>Perform the planned production restore.</li><li>Copy filesystem assets and test with the application account.</li><li>Clean test records by verified identifiers, not blanket truncation.</li></ol>\\n<p>The destination backup and rehearsal are easy to skip and especially valuable when something unexpected happens.</p>\\n<hr/>\\n<p><em>Incidents are retained from the October 2026 Zukatek draft; original logs are not available here for independent verification.</em></p>\\n<p>References: <a href=\\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/app-pgdump.html\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">PostgreSQL pg_dump</a>, <a href=\\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/app-pgrestore.html\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">PostgreSQL pg_restore</a>.</p>\\n<p><em>The cover is an AI-generated illustration, not a photograph of the actual system.</em></p>", "category_id": "527817a9-ade5-46d4-b64d-37d4bff2c875", "published_at": "2026-10-18T12:34:07.112183+07:00", "featured_path": "cms/articles/9d3b6d45-dec8-5495-b0be-674518abfdd3/featured-deded590-1845-589e-9366-d3b6041e2133.webp"}', '2026-10-18 12:34:07.112778+07', '2026-10-18 12:34:07.125978+07')
ON CONFLICT (entity, locale, slug) DO UPDATE SET
  entity_id = EXCLUDED.entity_id, is_current = EXCLUDED.is_current,
  search_text = EXCLUDED.search_text, body = EXCLUDED.body,
