INSERT INTO published_docs (entity, locale, slug, entity_id, is_current, search_text, body, published_at, written_at)
VALUES ('article', 'en', 'technology-priorities-small-businesses-2026', '3e9fc822-291a-5a63-a145-33d6191ccd59', true, 'Technology priorities for small businesses in 2026 Three changes that affect decisions in a company of 5–50 people, what to do first, and where the promises need closer scrutiny.', E'{"seo": {"title": "Technology in 2026: what small businesses should do first", "noindex": false, "keywords": "technology 2026, AI agents, small business, digital transformation", "nofollow": false, "og_title": "", "description": "Practical priorities for small businesses: reliable data, measured infrastructure improvements, and controlled AI automation.", "canonical_url": "", "og_description": ""}, "slug": "technology-priorities-small-businesses-2026", "title": "Technology priorities for small businesses in 2026", "excerpt": "Three changes that affect decisions in a company of 5–50 people, what to do first, and where the promises need closer scrutiny.", "og_path": null, "tag_ids": [], "body_html": "<p>Every December, the internet fills up with lists of next year&#39;s technology trends. Many are plausible but unhelpful: they describe a direction without telling you what to do on Monday morning.</p>\\n<p>This article takes a different approach. It focuses on changes that affect decisions in a company of 5–50 people, and suggests an order for addressing them.</p>\\n<h2>1. AI is moving from answering questions to taking action</h2>\\n<p>The significant change is not just smarter models. It is giving them permission to act.</p>\\n<p>A chatbot that answers a question is one thing. A system that looks up orders, creates contact records, or schedules appointments has a different risk profile and a different kind of usefulness.</p>\\n<p>You do not need an unverified adoption forecast to decide whether to use an agent. Evaluate the particular task, the permissions involved, and the damage an incorrect action could cause.</p>\\n<p>Once AI can act, its mistakes can become errors in your business records rather than a sentence someone reads and ignores.</p>\\n<p>This leads to a design principle from building our own assistant: <strong>control AI through architecture as well as instructions</strong>. If an assistant needs employee information, give it a controlled lookup tool. However, that alone does not stop it inventing names. The application must validate names and counts against the tool result, or render the approved records directly using a fixed template.</p>\\n<p>We discuss this in <a href=\\"/en/blog/reducing-chatbot-fabrications\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">the article on reducing fabricated chatbot answers</a>.</p>\\n<h3>What to do</h3>\\n<p>Start with a repetitive task that has clear rules and limited consequences if it goes wrong: summarizing material, classifying email, or drafting replies. After a sustained period of reliable operation—three months is a useful review point—consider whether it should receive write access.</p>\\n<h2>2. Measure infrastructure before buying more of it</h2>\\n<p>Five years ago, “we are too small to build our own software” could be a reasonable objection. Infrastructure costs alone are a less convincing explanation today.</p>\\n<p>The original project draft describes a public server-rendered website, an admin interface, an API, a database, and an AI assistant on a virtual server with four CPU cores and 2.9 GB of RAM. It records a load average of 0.06 at the time of measurement. That is an observation, not a capacity test.</p>\\n<p>The more useful point is where to look for a bottleneck. The draft records an internal response around 3 ms and an external request taking 100–130 ms. That suggests measuring the network and asset loading separately. Those different measurements do not establish that exactly 97% of the delay has one cause.</p>\\n<p>There is <strong>no basis for buying more CPU from these measurements alone</strong>, unless other evidence shows that CPU is the constraint.</p>\\n<h3>What to do</h3>\\n<p>Measure before upgrading. <a href=\\"/en/blog/server-optimization-measure-first\\" rel=\\"nofollow\\">Server optimization: measure first</a> explains the process and the limitations of the recorded figures.</p>\\n<h2>3. Reliable data matters more than another tool</h2>\\n<p>This is the least exciting part, and often the most important.</p>\\n<p>The useful tasks above depend on information being accurate, complete enough, and retrievable. If customer records are scattered across three spreadsheets, two mailboxes, and a notebook, AI will struggle to produce dependable answers. It may help classify the material, but people still need to check it and agree on a consistent source of record.</p>\\n<p>My suggested order is:</p>\\n<ol><li>Bring the data into a place where it can be queried.</li><li>Digitize recurring transactions: orders, contact requests, appointments.</li><li>Introduce AI for reading and summarizing.</li><li>Only then consider allowing it to write.</li></ol>\\n<p>Skipping directly from scattered data to autonomous actions is an expensive way to discover that the foundations were missing.</p>\\n<h2>A promise to examine carefully: “no-code replaces developers”</h2>\\n<p>Low-code and no-code platforms can be useful for forms, approval workflows, and dashboards. These are reasonable places to use them.</p>\\n<p>But a polished demonstration does not show every operational responsibility. The original project account describes problems such as certificate issuance, an AI provider running out of quota, incompatible PostgreSQL versions, and a CDN holding an outdated service worker.</p>\\n<p>These are examples of issues a no-code demonstration may not cover. They do not establish that every no-code platform is incapable of handling them.</p>\\n<p>Use such platforms for internal workflows where recovery is straightforward. For customer-facing features or payments, first examine access control, integration support, backups, and incident handling.</p>\\n<h2>Where to start next quarter</h2>\\n<p>If you can do only one thing, start by making your data consistent and retrievable. It may not make an exciting meeting presentation, but it supports everything else in this article. New tools still need someone to decide what the records mean and who is responsible for them.</p>\\n<hr/>\\n<p><em>Infrastructure figures are retained from the original Zukatek draft. This review had neither the original logs nor server access to verify them independently.</em></p>\\n<p><em>The cover is an AI-generated illustration, not a photograph of the actual system.</em></p>", "category_id": "527817a9-ade5-46d4-b64d-37d4bff2c875", "published_at": "2026-10-18T12:56:50.0677228+07:00", "featured_path": "cms/articles/3e9fc822-291a-5a63-a145-33d6191ccd59/featured-c0c4a688-151e-57c2-a88d-6adb7eceb3d5.webp"}', '2026-10-18 12:56:50.068145+07', '2026-10-18 12:56:50.076159+07')
