INSERT INTO published_docs (entity, locale, slug, entity_id, is_current, search_text, body, published_at, written_at)
VALUES ('article', 'en', 'three-small-team-problems', '1d064338-6db9-51ba-9517-f591436107b2', true, 'Three problems small teams face—and how to tackle them before buying software Scattered knowledge, repetitive work and missed customer inquiries often need a clearer process before they need another subscription.', E'{"seo": {"title": "Three small-team problems to solve before buying software", "noindex": false, "keywords": "small team management, productivity, automation, customer management", "nofollow": false, "og_title": "", "description": "Practical steps for shared knowledge, repetitive tasks and customer ownership in teams of 3–20 people.", "canonical_url": "", "og_description": ""}, "slug": "three-small-team-problems", "title": "Three problems small teams face—and how to tackle them before buying software", "excerpt": "Scattered knowledge, repetitive work and missed customer inquiries often need a clearer process before they need another subscription.", "og_path": null, "tag_ids": [], "body_html": "<p>Many small teams I have worked with encounter these three problems. Their symptoms differ, but buying software before fixing the process can simply create another disorganized place with a monthly fee.</p>\\n<h2>Problem 1: knowledge lives in people&#39;s heads</h2>\\n<h3>Symptoms</h3>\\n<ul><li>“Ask Nam; he knows about that.”</li><li>One person&#39;s leave brings a whole area of work to a halt.</li><li>Three people give three different answers to the same customer question.</li></ul>\\n<h3>Why it happens</h3>\\n<p>People are not necessarily hiding information. At the moment of capture, writing something down costs more effort than remembering it. The person who knows the answer does not need documentation, so the cost falls on someone who joins later.</p>\\n<h3>What to do</h3>\\n<p>Start with a smaller rule than “write documentation”: <strong>when answering a question for the second time, write the answer down before sending it</strong>.</p>\\n<p>The first question is ordinary conversation. The second is evidence that the answer will be useful again. This avoids asking everyone to predict in advance what deserves documentation.</p>\\n<h3>When a tool becomes useful</h3>\\n<p>When people can no longer find the answers already recorded. A clear symptom is someone asking a documented question because search failed them.</p>\\n<p>Choose for search quality, shareable links, easy editing, then appearance. Attractive pages do little good if nobody can retrieve the answer.</p>\\n<h2>Problem 2: small repetitive tasks quietly consume time</h2>\\n<h3>Symptoms</h3>\\n<ul><li>Copying data between places.</li><li>Opening five tabs each morning to check what happened overnight.</li><li>“It only takes two minutes.”</li></ul>\\n<p>Two minutes, five times a day, over 200 working days adds up to 2,000 minutes: <strong>more than 33 hours a year</strong>, for one person doing one task.</p>\\n<h3>Why it stays unsolved</h3>\\n<p>Each repetition feels cheap. The cost becomes visible only when someone adds it up.</p>\\n<h3>What to do</h3>\\n<p>For one week, record the tasks you do more than once. A task name and a count are enough to start. At the end of the week, multiply by the time per repetition.</p>\\n<p>Use the totals to identify the first candidate for improvement rather than trying to automate everything at once.</p>\\n<h3>Before automating</h3>\\n<p>The order is <strong>remove, simplify, then automate</strong>.</p>\\n<p>Before scripting a weekly report, ask who reads it. If nobody does, the better solution may be to stop producing it.</p>\\n<h2>Problem 3: customer inquiries fall through the gaps</h2>\\n<h3>Symptoms</h3>\\n<ul><li>A weekend inquiry is not seen until Monday.</li><li>A potential customer asks a question, but nobody owns the reply.</li><li>Nobody knows how many inquiries arrived last month.</li></ul>\\n<h3>Why it happens</h3>\\n<p>Messages arrive somewhere without a clear person responsible for checking it. A shared inbox is a familiar example: everyone can see it, so everyone assumes someone else is dealing with it.</p>\\n<p>The project draft describes contact and newsletter forms that saved records correctly but notified nobody. The information waited until someone remembered to open the admin screen.</p>\\n<h3>What to do, in order</h3>\\n<p><strong>Assign one owner per channel.</strong> Use a person&#39;s name, not “the team.” Ownership can rotate weekly, but it should be clear at any given time.</p>\\n<p><strong>Notify proactively.</strong> Do not rely on someone remembering to check a screen. Define coverage hours and a fallback if the alert is missed.</p>\\n<p><strong>Count.</strong> Track inquiries received, replies made and response time. Without measurements it is hard to know whether the process is working.</p>\\n<h3>Keep alerts useful</h3>\\n<p>Group related messages. Five lines from a customer may be five machine events but one conversation to a person. Too many alerts teach people to turn them off.</p>\\n<p>Send alerts to people who can act on them and have permission to open the record. An alert leading to a locked screen is rarely useful.</p>\\n<h2>A suggested order</h2>\\n<p>If all three apply:</p>\\n<ol><li>Start with missed inquiries: they can directly affect revenue, and assigning an owner can be inexpensive.</li><li>Address repetitive work next to release time for other improvements.</li><li>Build shared knowledge steadily; its value often appears over a longer period.</li></ol>\\n<p>Change this order when your own costs or risks point elsewhere.</p>\\n<h2>When to buy software</h2>\\n<p>Answer three questions first:</p>\\n<ol><li>What is the current process? Can you describe it on one page?</li><li>Which steps should change or disappear before digitization?</li><li>What is the specific problem, and how will you measure improvement?</li></ol>\\n<p>“We should look more professional” is not yet a defined business problem. “Three inquiries a week go unanswered, each with an estimated value” gives you a problem to solve and a way to evaluate the result.</p>\\n<hr/>\\n<p><em>Project situations are retained from the supplied draft and were not independently verified from logs. The proposed priorities are suggestions; adapt them to your team&#39;s costs and risks.</em></p>\\n<p><em>The cover is an AI-generated illustration, not a photograph of the actual system.</em></p>", "category_id": "527817a9-ade5-46d4-b64d-37d4bff2c875", "published_at": "2026-10-18T12:57:13.017315+07:00", "featured_path": "cms/articles/1d064338-6db9-51ba-9517-f591436107b2/featured-724f0c7e-2c08-5f09-b044-689137d3d979.webp"}', '2026-10-18 12:57:13.017841+07', '2026-10-18 12:57:13.028676+07')
